{"timestamp":"2026-04-24T13:09:11.861Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"Best approach:\n\n1. Detect shallowness with Git, not by reading `.git/shallow` directly.\n2. Only use `--unshallow` on the next full checkpoint fetch, not on every fetch.\n3. Keep it out of the tree-only / push-sync fast paths.\n\n**Detection**\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\ngit rev-parse --is-shallow-repository\n```\n\nWhy this is best:\n- It is Git’s supported API.\n- It works correctly with worktrees and indirect gitdirs.\n- It avoids path assumptions around `.git/shallow`.\n\nA helper would look like:\n\n```go\nfunc isShallowRepository(ctx context.Context, repoDir string) (bool, error) {\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, \"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"--is-shallow-repository\")\n\tcmd.Dir = repoDir\n\tout, err := cmd.Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn false, fmt.Errorf(\"git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(out)) == \"true\", nil\n}\n```\n\n**Where to apply `--unshallow`**\n\nDo it only in the full fetch paths:\n- `FetchMetadataBranch`\n- `FetchV2MainRef`\n- checkpoint-remote full-fetch equivalents\n\nDo not do it in:\n- `FetchMetadataTreeOnly`\n- `FetchV2MainTreeOnly`\n- push recovery / reconcile fetches\n- blob-by-hash fetches\n\nReason: `--unshallow` is expensive and changes repo state globally. It should be tied to the first fetch that already intends to bring in full data.\n\n**Implementation shape**\n\nAdd an `Unshallow bool` to `remote.FetchOptions`, mapping to `git fetch --unshallow`.\n\nThen in the full fetch helpers:\n\n1. Resolve repo path.\n2. Check `isShallowRepository(...)`.\n3. If `true`, pass `Unshallow: true` on that fetch.\n\nExample shape:\n\n```go\nshallow, err := isShallowRepository(ctx, repoPath)\nif err != nil {\n\treturn err\n}\n\noutput, fetchErr := remote.Fetch(ctx, remote.FetchOptions{\n\tRemote:     fetchTarget,\n\tRefSpecs:   []string{refSpec},\n\tNoTags:     true,\n\tNoFilter:   true,\n\tUnshallow:  shallow,\n})\n```\n\nAnd in `remote.Fetch`:\n\n```go\nif opts.Unshallow {\n\targs = append(args, \"--unshallow\")\n}\n```\n\n**Why this is the best tradeoff**\n\n- Minimal surface area.\n- Self-heals old shallow repos.\n- Avoids slowing down normal sync logic.\n- Doesn’t require a one-off migration command.\n\n**One caution**\n\nIf a repo is shallow because the user intentionally cloned the main repo shallow, `--unshallow` will deepen the whole repository, not just checkpoint refs in any narrow conceptual sense. That may be acceptable, but it is a product decision. If you only want to repair legacy checkpoint-created shallowness, you’d need extra state/marker logic, which is more complex.\n\nSo the pragmatic answer is:\n- detect with `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository`\n- apply `--unshallow` once, on the next full checkpoint fetch path only.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
